    "description": "The Policy Administration system edits claims across several repositories and service methods. Every request must acquire the relevant data protection before reading or changing it and release it only after the request completes. Today, controllers and repository helpers acquire guards inconsistently: some report paths acquire them, scheduled jobs do not, and a newly added endpoint can forget the required step. Unit tests pass because they exercise methods sequentially, while concurrent users occasionally observe a protected record during an unfinished operation or trigger a late conflict after expensive work. The design must make it impossible for an application method to omit the acquisition, and it must keep the guard active across all database calls made during the business operation. A central request pipeline or shared application supertype is being considered so that acquisition and release are enforced uniformly, including for non-HTTP entry points.",
    "decision_statement": "Place acquisition and release of required data protection in a mandatory framework or shared supertype boundary so every business operation follows the same policy.",
    "notes": "Framework-enforced acquisition closes the per-entry-path gaps that sequential unit tests cannot catch; the pessimistic and coarse-grained siblings cover the guard-type axis the scenario leaves open."
  },
